      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Focused on plans for the evening, The characters discuss their situation. I'm so sorry. I didn't mean to. Let's listen closely to Chef Viktor and Elena's exchange in Russian first, and then walk through each line together.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Что же это такое?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Что у вас за ресторан?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Простите, я не хотела.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Какие планы на вечер?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "У меня никаких.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "А что, есть варианты?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вариант один.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Но нужно быть вдвоём.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Какой тонкий намёк.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Я хотела поехать за покупками.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Но твой вариант мне нравится.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Отлично.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let us examine the conversation line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Что же это такое?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What is this?' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be' here, linking 'what' directly with 'but', combining 'what' with 'but' and 'this'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Что у вас за ресторан?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What kind of restaurant is this?' Russian leaves out 'am', 'is', or 'are' in the present tense, linking 'what' straight to 'restaurant'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Простите, я не хотела.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I'm sorry, I didn't mean to.' The '-a' on the end of the past-tense verb for 'wanted' tells you the subject is feminine. Chef Viktor answers straight away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Какие планы на вечер?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Any plans for tonight?' Russian leaves out 'am', 'is', or 'are' in the present tense, linking 'plans' straight to 'evening'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "У меня никаких.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I don't have any.' To express possession, Russian uses the preposition 'by' plus the genitive pronoun followed by 'no ... at all'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "А что, есть варианты?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Got something in mind?' Russian forms this yes-or-no question without a question word, relying on rising pitch on 'option'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вариант один.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'One thing.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be' here, linking 'option' directly with 'one', combining 'option' with 'one'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Но нужно быть вдвоём.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'But it takes two.' Chef Barinov expresses this naturally by combining the word for 'necessary' with 'be' and 'the two of us together'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Какой тонкий намёк.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What a subtle hint.' The question word for 'what a' comes right at the start, followed directly by 'hint'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Я хотела поехать за покупками.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I wanted to go shopping.' Elena chooses the imperfective verb meaning 'wanted' for the ongoing action, followed by 'to go' and 'purchase'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Но твой вариант мне нравится.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'But I like your idea.' Notice the reflexive ending on the verb for 'is pleasing', which directs the action back onto the subject.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Отлично.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Excellent.' Chef Barinov delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'perfect'. Now let's listen to the whole conversation again from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
